@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# Northwind
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Go-live | 2026-W44 (status `onboarding` in the registry until the account team moves it to live) |
+| Contract | `docs/NORTHWIND_SOW.md` |
+| Grain / timezone | 30 minutes / America/Chicago |
+| Operating hours | 06:00–24:00 local, seven days; `vip` 08:00–20:00 |
+| Queues | priority, standard, vip |
+| Config | `configs/northwind.yaml` (schema 1) |
+| Roster | `data/agents/northwind.csv`, 51 agents (8 part-time); 11 hold `vip` |
+| Data | `data/northwind/export_<ISO>.csv`, local time with a UTC offset per row (`data/northwind/README.txt`) |
+
+## Data drop
+
+The export is used as delivered: `wfm.ingest` derives UTC from each row's `utc_offset`. A DST week
+has 334 or 338 half-hours. Off-grid timestamps (2026-W44 has two rows at `10:01`) are assigned to the
+interval they fall in, with a warning on stderr. Report such rows to Northwind's WFM contact.
+
+## Run
+
+`python -m wfm run --client northwind --week <week>` writes `schedule.csv`, `eval.json`, `RUNBOOK.md`
+and `exceptions.md` under `out/northwind/`. It takes about 30 s because the week scheduler
+(`wfm/scheduler/week.py`) searches for the best week. The handover note in the RUNBOOK comes from
+`docs/clients/northwind_handover.md`.
+
+## Contract points the service does not have elsewhere
+
+Interval AHT and the occupancy cap in staffing, attainment over every operating interval, 11 h rest,
+split shifts for PT only, breaks at the largest gross surplus within 2–5 h, a vip floor of one agent
+06:00–24:00, and an efficiency bound of 1.57 × required hours. See the config comments.
+
+## Known limit
+
+The vip roster (11 agents, at most 440 h a week) cannot reach 0.95 weekly attainment. The schedule
+reaches about 0.89–0.92, and `exceptions.md` records the figure every week.
